          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly presents Goneril's loving emotional state toward Lear as true. Its force is a clear avowal, regardless of whether that avowal is sincere.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The focal words do not undertake a future action, obligation, or relational commitment. Goneril answers Lear's request to state how much she loves him; Lear subsequently grants land, but that response does not turn her avowal into a promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs as a composed answer in a formal, competitive declaration rather than as a reflex-like affective discharge. Its core force remains assertive avowal, even though the surrounding speech is emotionally heightened.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"A love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"No residual core force beyond the avowal is adequately supported. The expression may function rhetorically within Lear's test and seek a favorable response, but the words themselves primarily present love as an existing state rather than performing another distinct act.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"That we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a formal court setting, Lear asks his daughters to declare which loves him most while announcing that his largest bounty will follow the greatest professed love. Goneril, speaking first, gives the focal avowal as the opening of an elaborate declaration.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression contains no identifiable undertaking by Goneril. Lear immediately rewards her declaration with territory, which is uptake of her answer in the love test, not acceptance of a promise. The later supplied passages portray grave wrongdoing by the elder sisters toward Lear, giving evidence against the avowal's sincerity or accuracy, but they do not retrospectively change its original avowing force.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as William Shakespeare's King Lear, specifically the opening love test and later reconciliation and final scenes.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied evidence strongly establishes the utterance's avowing function and its strategic setting, but it does not directly disclose Goneril's inner emotional state at the moment of speaking. Later conduct supports skepticism about sincerity, while the target itself professes affection; accordingly, actual-affection evidence is classified as mixed rather than treated as settled.\"}"
        }
